CAT = "cpctaEkg"; SEG = "EKG"
# Domain 5 tasks 5A to 5I; knowledge k118 to k131.
Q = [
("Where on the arms and legs are limb electrodes placed?",
 "On fleshy areas, not over bone", ["Directly over the joints", "On the palms and soles", "Over large tattoos"],
 "Limb electrodes go on flat, fleshy areas of the limbs, which gives better contact and less muscle and movement artifact than bony spots or joints."),
("Using the color code common in the United States for limb leads, which electrode is white?",
 "Right arm", ["Left arm", "Left leg", "Right leg"],
 "In that color code, right arm is white, left arm is black, left leg is red and right leg is green."),
("For a 12-lead EKG on a patient with a below-knee amputation, where do the two leg electrodes go?",
 "On both thighs, at equal height", ["On the abdomen near the navel", "On the chest at the V6 spot", "It is left off entirely"],
 "Limb electrodes can be moved up the limb, but both legs or both arms should be placed at matching levels so the tracing stays balanced."),
("What prepares the skin for good electrode contact?",
 "Clean and dry it well", ["Apply lotion to soften it", "Wet it with water just before", "Dust it with talcum powder"],
 "Oil, lotion, moisture and thick hair weaken contact. The skin is cleaned, dried and lightly abraded or clipped as needed."),
("Before a 12-lead EKG, why does the technician ask the patient to put her phone away?",
 "It can cause interference", ["It slows the paper speed", "It changes her heart rate", "It drains the machine battery"],
 "Phones and other electronic devices near the leads can add electrical artifact, so they are moved away during the recording."),
("Where does the normal heartbeat start?",
 "The sinoatrial (SA) node", ["The atrioventricular (AV) node", "The bundle of His", "The Purkinje fibers"],
 "The SA node in the right atrium is the natural pacemaker. The impulse then travels to the AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches and Purkinje fibers."),
("On an EKG, what does the P wave show?",
 "Atrial depolarization", ["Ventricular depolarization", "Ventricular repolarization", "Atrial repolarization only"],
 "The P wave is the atria depolarizing. The QRS complex is the ventricles depolarizing, and the T wave is ventricular repolarization."),
("What is the standard EKG paper speed?",
 "25 mm per second", ["50 mm per second", "10 mm per second", "5 mm per second"],
 "Standard paper speed is 25 mm/s. A faster 50 mm/s speed spreads out the waves when a fast rhythm is hard to read."),
("A box of electrodes was left open for weeks and the gel looks dry. What does the technician do?",
 "Use a fresh pack", ["Wet them with tap water", "Press them on harder", "Use them on the limbs only"],
 "Dried gel gives poor skin contact and artifact. Electrodes are kept sealed, used before their expiration date and replaced when dry."),
("A patient's tracing wanders up and down. The technician sees lotion on her chest. What fixes it?",
 "Clean the skin, new electrodes", ["Turn the gain up to 20", "Change the paper speed", "Ask her to hold her breath"],
 "Lotion and poor contact cause a wandering baseline. Cleaning the skin and applying fresh electrodes fixes the cause instead of hiding it."),
("A patient with a constant hand tremor needs an EKG. Where can the arm electrodes go to reduce artifact?",
 "Higher on the upper arms", ["On the fingers", "On the back of the hands", "On the chest at V1 and V2"],
 "Limb electrodes may be placed closer to the trunk, such as on the upper arms, where tremor causes less movement. Both arms are placed at matching levels."),
("A cold, shivering patient's tracing shows jagged, irregular baseline movement. What does the technician do?",
 "Warm him and help him relax", ["Increase the paper speed", "Move the chest leads lower", "Print it and stop there"],
 "Somatic (muscle) tremor artifact comes from shivering or tensing. A blanket, a comfortable position and relaxed limbs reduce it."),
("An alert patient on telemetry has a run of fast, wide complexes and says he feels dizzy. What does the technician do?",
 "Stay with him and alert the nurse", ["Reapply the electrodes", "Print the strip for the chart", "Leave to get the nurse"],
 "A fast, wide rhythm with symptoms may be ventricular tachycardia with a pulse. The patient is not left alone, and the nurse is alerted at once."),
("The tracing becomes a chaotic, wavy line with no clear complexes. The patient is unresponsive. What does the technician do first?",
 "Check him and start CPR", ["Fix the electrodes first", "Print a longer strip", "Note it and wait for the nurse"],
 "A chaotic line in an unresponsive patient suggests ventricular fibrillation. The patient is checked first, help is called and CPR begins; artifact is checked only if the patient is responsive."),
("A telemetry box gives a low-battery alert. What does the technician do?",
 "Change the battery promptly", ["Finish rounds, then change it", "Silence the alert and chart it", "Report it at shift change"],
 "A dead battery stops monitoring, so the battery is replaced quickly per policy and the monitor tech or nurse is told the patient was briefly off the monitor."),
("In which chamber of the heart is the SA node found?",
 "Right atrium", ["Left atrium", "Right ventricle", "Left ventricle"],
 "The sinoatrial node sits in the upper wall of the right atrium, near where the superior vena cava enters."),
("A patient on telemetry is going to shower. What does the technician check first?",
 "Whether removal is approved", ["That the box stays on the gown", "That the leads are tucked in", "That the alarms are paused"],
 "The monitor is removed or covered only as the nurse or policy allows, so the patient is not left unmonitored without approval."),
("A patient on telemetry wants to walk the hall. What does the technician check?",
 "That he stays in range", ["That the leads are removed", "That the alarms are turned off", "That the battery is taken out"],
 "Telemetry sends the rhythm wirelessly to a central station, so the patient stays within the unit's range and the nurse knows he is walking."),
("Before a portable EKG machine is used, what is checked?",
 "Charge, cables and paper supply", ["The gain set to 20 mm/mV", "The paper speed set to 50 mm/s", "The filter switched off"],
 "A portable machine needs a charged battery, intact cables and paper. Frayed cables are taken out of service and reported to biomedical engineering."),
("A telemetry alarm shows a fast, jagged rhythm while the patient is vigorously brushing his teeth. What does the technician do first?",
 "Look at the patient", ["Start chest compressions", "Reset the alarm limits", "Replace all the electrodes"],
 "Movement can mimic a dangerous rhythm. The technician first checks the patient: if he is alert and well, it is likely artifact, and the nurse or monitor tech is told."),
]
